node_modules/
.env
# …and every other name Next loads. `.env` is the only one this machine has
# today, which is exactly why the line above looked complete. `next dev`, `next
# build` and `next start` also read `.env.local`, `.env.production`,
# `.env.development` and `.env.*.local` — so the first person to create one for
# a local override has a live secrets file that nothing here matches.
#
# `.dockerignore` has said `.env.*` since the container went up on 2026-09-09,
# and the reasoning is written out at length there: a secret copied into a build
# context is baked into an image layer and stays readable from the old layer
# even after a later commit deletes it. Git is the same shape of mistake with a
# longer memory. The two files disagreeing WAS the gap — what may not go into
# an image may not go into the history either.
#
# What is in these files on this machine: `MONGODB_URI`, `JWT_SECRET`, and
# `TUNNEL_TOKEN`, which is the credential the public Cloudflare URL runs on and
# lives nowhere else (docs/docker.md). A leaked one is rotated at the dashboard,
# not deleted from a commit.
#
# `.env.example` is tracked on purpose — it is the documentation of which keys
# exist — and the negation below is what keeps it that way.
.env.*
!.env.example
*.log

# `npm run backup` output. A complete copy of the database, which means
# passwordHash for every account and birthDate for every employee — the field
# the roster deliberately hides from managers. It does not go where the code
# goes. Keep backups off this disk entirely; ./backups is only the default
# landing spot, and `--out` is there to move them.
backups/
# …and wherever else a backup is told to land. The line above matches a NAME,
# but the destination is a SETTING: `--out` on `npm run backup`, or `BACKUP_DIR`
# read by `backupDestination()` in lib/backupStatusQuery.js. Both are resolved
# against the process's working directory, which for anybody running npm here is
# this folder — so `npm run backup -- --out ot-backups` writes the whole roster
# into the repo under a name the line above does not match, and the comment
# above says to use `--out` in the same breath.
#
# Anchored to the root because that is the only place a relative `--out` or
# `BACKUP_DIR` can land, and unanchored it would silently swallow a future
# `lib/backup/`. A glob rather than the names somebody might pick, for the same
# reason `.next-*/` below is one: the next name is covered before it is used,
# not after 1185 files are already staged.
/*backup*/

.next/
# …and every scratch build beside it. `next.config.js` commits
# `distDir: process.env.VERIFY_DIST_DIR || '.next'`, which is how a build gets
# verified, or a second dev server run, without touching the one `next start`
# is serving — see README §Setup. The output lands in the repo root under
# whatever name that variable was given, and `.next/` above does not match it.
#
# `.next-verify` and `.next-dev3002` were untracked-but-not-ignored until
# 2026-08-26, when a `git add -A` staged 1185 of their files — including six
# Turbopack cache `.sst` blobs of 14–17MB each — into a commit about CSS. The
# glob rather than the two names, so the next scratch dir somebody invents is
# covered before it is used.
.next-*/
next-env.d.ts

# Per-machine Claude Code permissions. Tracked for two commits by mistake —
# it is a record of which shell commands one developer approved on one laptop,
# so it conflicts on every pull and tells nobody anything. `.claude/settings.json`
# (shared, deliberate) is NOT ignored.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Scratch scripts an agent wrote while walking the app — one-off mongo
# queries, mock-data builders, and `tokens.json`, which holds SIGNED JWTs
# minted for real accounts so a headless browser could open the app as them.
# A token in the history is a working credential in the history, and these
# are throwaway by construction: nothing here is meant to be read twice.
.tmp-edits/
